@model IReadOnlyList<EliteIndustries.Models.Product>
@using EliteIndustries.Models
@using EliteIndustries.Services
@*
    Homepage products overview band.

    A curated grid, not the catalogue. The cards themselves are the shared
    component in Views/Shared/_ProductCard.cshtml — this file owns only the
    band: its head, the grid it lays the cards out in, and the way out to the
    full listing.

    WHERE THE SIX COME FROM
    -----------------------
    HomeController passes ProductCatalog.Featured(6) — the products flagged
    IsFeatured, in catalogue order, capped at six. The count is the
    controller's decision and is NOT re-sliced here: a view that quietly took
    a different number than it was handed would make the controller a lie.

    Six because the grid is three across, so six is two clean rows. Seven
    products carry the flag; the seventh is one row short of a third row and
    would leave a gap the eye reads as a missing card.

    THE HEAD COUNTS ITSELF
    ----------------------
    The lede states how much of the range is on screen, and both figures are
    read — @Model.Count from what was actually passed in, and the catalogue
    total from ProductCatalog.All. Change Featured(6) to Featured(9) and the
    sentence follows on its own rather than going quietly stale.

    THE ENTRANCE
    ------------
    .ei-reveal is on the grid cells, not on the card. The card is shared and
    has no business deciding how it arrives on a page it does not own; the band
    laying it out does. reveal.js drives it and unobserves each cell as it
    lands, so there is no observer here — theme.css says in as many words that
    a section must not write a second one.

    The step is 70ms. Six cards at the 150ms the split bands use would leave
    the last card arriving nearly a second after the first, which stops reading
    as a stagger and starts reading as the page being slow.
*@

<section class="ei-band ei-band--shell ei-feat" aria-labelledby="ei-feat-title">
    <div class="ei-shell">

        <div class="ei-band-head ei-reveal">
            <p class="ei-eyebrow">What we make</p>

            <h2 id="ei-feat-title">Stocked, specified, ready to ship</h2>

            <p class="ei-lede">
                @Model.Count of the @ProductCatalog.All.Count lines we manufacture and hold in
                @CompanyProfile.City. Each one is specified against the leg it has to survive —
                the hold, the stack, and the handling at the far end.
            </p>
        </div>

        @*  A list, because that is what it is: a set of products of equal
            standing, in a deliberate order. The <li> carries the entrance and
            the card fills it.  *@
        <ul class="ei-feat-grid">
            @{ var step = 0; }
            @foreach (var product in Model)
            {
                <li class="ei-feat-cell ei-reveal" style="--d:@(step * 70)ms">
                    <partial name="_ProductCard" model="product" />
                </li>

                step++;
            }
        </ul>

        <div class="ei-feat-more ei-reveal">
            @*  Sentence case, matching "Explore products" in the hero and the
                "View all products" row already in the masthead's Products
                dropdown. The same destination should not be spelled two ways
                on one page.  *@
            <a class="ei-btn ei-btn--outline" asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Index">
                View all products
                <svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="#i-arrow-right" /></svg>
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
